"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 8.4 and an ISUP grade group 2 Gleason pattern from the prostatectomy specimen. Positive findings include negative surgical margins and no seminal vesicle invasion. Countervailing points are the absence of nodal sampling, which widens uncertainty regarding nodal status. The biopsy history shows a discrepancy, with the initial biopsy being Gleason 0+0 compared to the post-surgery finding of Gleason 3+4.\n\nThe prognosis would be significantly revised by postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory. Further information on any potential recurrence or changes in PSA kinetics is necessary for a more defined assessment. The current data lacks nodal status confirmation due to no nodes being removed, which limits prognostic certainty. The fixed estimated horizon is 76.69 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 49.85–103.54 months; its coverage has not been validated."
